// CLOSED 2026-09-24 (squeue #92): a partially moved array drops its units (runs of untouched elements, partially moved elements) from the last to the first, a run forward — rustc's order.
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row let_array_pattern_remainder_group_order_run (tier 1, run 1). AFTER A PARTIAL
// MOVE OUT OF AN ARRAY, THE ELEMENTS THE PATTERN DID NOT BIND ARE DESTROYED IN THE WRONG GROUP ORDER.
//
// `let [_, y, _] = arr;` over `[D; 3]`: rustc destroys y (2), then index 2 (3), then index 0 (1) —
// n = 231, exit 0 (MEASURED rustc 1.98.1, twin r02.rs). TODAY n = 213: the COUNT is right (every
// element destroyed exactly once) and the ORDER is not — Logos walks the remainder in forward index
// order 0 then 2, rustc emits the group AFTER the moved index before the group before it.
// ⚠ NOT a leak and NOT a double free: this row was invisible until round 2026-09-16j-arrpath2 fixed
// the count, and no count-based oracle can see it. Opened BY that round, which declined to fix it
// (reason 3): the arm is a drop-ELABORATION ordering change, unpriced, and co-landing it would
// smuggle an unpriced change inside a priced one.
// ⚠ THE SPEC CLAUSE IS SILENT, NOT WRONG. `expr.drop.tuple-array-index-order`
// (docs/spec/divergences.md) says a fixed array drops its elements in index order and that statically
// moved-out positions are suppressed; it says NOTHING about the order of the surviving groups, so
// this row contradicts no rule. MEASURED rustc controls, same round: a FULLY-owned [D;3] is 123
// (forward, and Logos agrees — the control does not move); `let [y,_,_]` is 123 and `let [_,_,y]` is
// 312, both forward remainders. Only a moved index with survivors on BOTH sides separates them.
// Same fact, other spellings measured wrong the same way: `match arr { [.., W{a:x,b:_}] }` 3124 vs
// rustc 3412; `match arr { [W{a:x,b:_}, ..] }` 1234 vs 1342.
package let_array_pattern_remainder_group_order_run;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c * 10i64 + self.v; } } }
struct W { a: D, b: D }
fn g(p: *mut i64) -> i64 {
    let arr: [D; 3] = [D { v: 1i64, c: p }, D { v: 2i64, c: p }, D { v: 3i64, c: p }];
    let [_, y, _] = arr;
    return y.v;
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    let k: i64 = g(p);
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    unsafe { printf("k=%ld n=%ld\n".as_ptr(), k, got); }
    if k != 2i64 { return 2i32; }
    if got != 231i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
